    {
      "id": "anomaly-baselines-and-climate-indices",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "methods/anomaly-baselines-and-climate-indices.md",
      "sha256": "e0afb5c187aca1e075f9549153b1e896320bb0539544f66d3293fab4b4b77c92",
      "reason": "The whole task is an anomaly index (Nino 3.4, ONI) defined against a monthly climatology, so this fragment fixes how the anomaly is formed relative to its own baseline and explains why series on 1991-2020 and 1981-2010 differ by a constant offset.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Build RECON_9120 as the anomaly of ERSSTv5 against its own monthly climatology of each calendar month (`compute_anomaly_vs_own_climatology`), not against an observed climatology (`compute_anomaly_vs_woa23`); Niño 3.4 is the standard region. Two series on different baselines differ by one constant per calendar month even when their variability is identical, so the RECON_8110-minus-RECON_9120 offset is not an error; state the climatology and baseline used.",
        "Count events by persistence, not by eye: a phase counts only when the series stays beyond the threshold for the minimum number of consecutive steps (`min_duration` of `detect_threshold_events`); a single step beyond the threshold is noise."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "omip-weighted-means-and-integrals",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/omip-weighted-means-and-integrals.md",
      "sha256": "89de52676ae54db8f5897bc77a08c78de3f776696db0020935a38b790c302cbe",
      "reason": "The reconstruction averages SST over the 5S-5N, 170W-120W box, and this fragment constrains how a surface field is averaged from gridded cells (area weighting), which changes the reconstructed numbers and how they must be stated.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "The 5S-5N, 170W-120W mean must be area-weighted (`compute_basic_stat` with `op='area_weighted_mean'`); a plain `mean()` weights every element equally, which is wrong as well on a regular grid over latitude as on a curvilinear grid."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "serial-correlation-and-significance",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/serial-correlation-and-significance.md",
      "sha256": "fe11bd008257a589f44e369faba5fa31ab3cf9ad99280e88c763ac45d2b0e917",
      "reason": "The reported Pearson correlation is between two strongly autocorrelated monthly series, so this fragment governs how that correlation (and any p-value) may be quoted.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "RECON_9120 and the CPC series are two estimates of the same quantity: correlate them as they are, not with `detrend=True` — their shared trend is part of the agreement being checked, not a spurious link.",
        "If a significance is quoted for that correlation, use the effective sample size from the lag-one autocorrelation rather than the number of months; a monthly ocean series is strongly autocorrelated."
      ]
    },
